Let A be a finite alphabet with |A| ≥ 2 and let A* denote the set of finite words over A. A pattern is a word p over an alphabet of variables E = {x_1, x_2, ...}. A word w ∈ A* is said to encounter the pattern p if there exists a non-erasing morphism h : E* → A* such that h(p) occurs as a factor of w; otherwise w avoids p. The pattern p is k-avoidable if there exists an infinite word over a k-letter alphabet that avoids p, and avoidable if it is k-avoidable for some k; the avoidability index λ(p) is the least such k (and ∞ if p is unavoidable).

Develop and apply probabilistic-method techniques (such as the Lovász Local Lemma, entropy compression, the cluster-expansion / Moser–Tardos framework, or related random constructions) to obtain new results in pattern avoidance for words. Concretely, formulate and prove probabilistic-method theorems that yield improved bounds on quantities such as:

(a) the avoidability index λ(p) of a given avoidable pattern p, or general upper bounds on λ(p) in terms of structural parameters of p (e.g., length, number of distinct variables, or maximum exponent);

(b) the growth rate (entropy) of the language of words over a k-letter alphabet avoiding a given pattern or set of patterns, in particular sharper lower bounds showing the language is exponentially large;

(c) thresholds for avoidability of fractional powers, abelian patterns, k-abelian patterns, or formulas, including determination or improved estimates of the repetition threshold for new classes of patterns.

Provide a unified probabilistic framework for pattern avoidance over words and use it to settle (or quantitatively advance) at least one previously open instance among (a)–(c).

